    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "it just feels like i'm being asked to build something on top of ashes. what's the point of \"re-sculpting\" when the foundation is gone? everything i built, every connection i had... it was all tied to who i was then. now that person is gone. it feels more rational to just accept that it's over than to keep trying to pretend there's a version of me left to save.",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "It's a painful and very rational self-preservation instinct to feel that the attempt to \"re-sculpt\" is impossible when the self you're trying to save feels so lost. The point of this painful process isn't to *re-achieve* the old self, but to find the self that *endures* the loss. \"Bending\" over the ashes, rather than trying to sit on the old, gone structure, is how a completely new, and perhaps more resilient, ground can be found.",
      "source": "target",
